.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt ADDUSER 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm adduser
.Nd create a user account
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl DSqh
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Op Fl g Ar group
.Op Fl G Ar group , ...
.Op Fl -admin
.Op Fl c Ar "full name"
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Fl w Cm no | none | yes
.Op Fl p Ar hash
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Ar name
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
creates a user account in the DirectoryServices property lists under
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices ,
which is where NextBSD keeps its regular users.
System accounts stay in
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
and are created with
.Xr pw 8 .
.Pp
Run with no arguments,
.Nm
asks six questions:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Username: joe
Full name: Joe Maloney
Shell [/bin/zsh]:
Administrator? (can use sudo) [no]: yes
Password (empty for none):
Password again:
.Ed
.Pp
then prints a summary, asks for confirmation, and offers to add another
account.
.Pp
Given a
.Ar name
on the command line,
.Nm
asks nothing: every field not supplied by a flag takes its default.
The
password is the one exception, because defaulting it silently would be
wrong; supply
.Fl p ,
.Fl w Cm none
or
.Fl w Cm no ,
or run without a name to be asked.
.Ss What is not asked
NextBSD's property lists carry no home directory, no login class and no
password ageing, so there is nothing to ask about them:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
The home is always
.Pa /Local/Users/ Ns Ar name ,
computed on lookup.
There is no
.Fl d
or
.Fl M .
.It
There is no login class, so no
.Fl L .
.It
There is no expiry or ageing.
.El
.Pp
The home is built from
.Pa /System/Library/User Template
by
.Xr createhomedir 8 ,
which
.Nm
runs after the account exists.
If that fails the account is still created;
run
.Nm createhomedir
again by hand.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-w no|none|yes"
.It Fl -admin
Make the account an administrator, which means adding it to the
.Li admin
group.
That group is what the shipped
.Xr sudoers 5
grants, so the account can use
.Xr sudo 8 .
Equivalent to
.Fl G Ar admin .
.It Fl c Ar "full name"
The real name, stored as
.Li realName .
.It Fl D
Do not create the home directory.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read accounts from
.Ar file ,
or from standard input when it is
.Fl .
See
.Sx BATCH FILES .
.It Fl G Ar group , ...
Additional groups.
Each must already exist in the directory.
.It Fl g Ar group
The primary group, by name or gid.
It must already exist.
Without this,
.Nm
creates a private group with the same name and gid as the user.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl p Ar hash
Use an already-hashed password, as
.Xr crypt 3
produces.
For an installer or a provisioning script; nothing is prompted.
.It Fl q
Print only errors.
The summary and the confirmation are suppressed.
.It Fl S
Do not check the shell against
.Xr shells 5 .
.It Fl s Ar shell
The login shell.
The default is
.Pa /bin/zsh .
Checked against
.Pa /etc/shells
unless
.Fl S
is given.
.It Fl u Ar uid
The exact uid.
Without this the first free uid at or above 5001 is used.
The gid matches unless
.Fl g
says otherwise.
.It Fl w Cm no | none | yes
How the account authenticates.
.Cm none
sets
.Li noPassword ,
so it logs in with no password, which is how the seeded
.Li admin
account works.
.Cm no
stores an unmatchable hash, so no password works and the account cannot log
in until one is set.
.Cm yes ,
the default, prompts, or takes
.Fl p .
.El
.Pp
Two flags are accepted and ignored, so a script written for another BSD
still runs:
.Fl k ,
because homes come from the user template rather than a skeleton directory,
and
.Fl m ,
because NextBSD ships no mail transport to send a welcome message with.
.Sh IDENTITY RANGES
.Bl -column "5001 and above" "description" -offset indent
.It Sy Range Ta Sy Use
.It 0-999 Ta system accounts, in Pa /etc/master.passwd
.It 1000-4999 Ta unused, a deliberate gap
.It 5000 Ta the Li admin group, and the account seeded with the image
.It 5001+ Ta regular users and their private groups
.El
.Sh BATCH FILES
A batch file holds one account per line, six colon-separated fields:
.Pp
.Dl name:uid:gid:gecos:shell:password
.Pp
An empty
.Va uid
or
.Va gid
means allocate one.
The
.Va password
field is a
.Em hash ,
not a plaintext password, the same as
.Fl p .
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored.
.Pp
Six fields rather than the ten a batch file carries on other BSDs.
The four
left out name things NextBSD's property lists cannot hold, and keeping them
as empty columns would suggest they might work.
A first line of
.Pp
.Dl #format: nextbsd-1
.Pp
is accepted, and records which format the file is in so a later one can be
told apart.
An unrecognised format is an error rather than a guess.
.Pp
A line that fails is reported and the rest of the file is still processed.
The exit status is non-zero if any line failed.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
Present when this machine is the directory server, in which case
.Nm
manages the accounts here and never consults
.Pa /Network .
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/Domain.plist
Present when this machine is bound to a server, in which case
.Nm
refuses: the account belongs on the server.
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
Names that server for the message.
It does not decide the role.
.It Pa /System/Library/User Template
What a new home is built from.
.It Pa /etc/shells
.It Pa /etc/login.conf
.Va passwd_format
here chooses the hash format.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 4n -compact
.It 0
The account was created.
.It 1
Usage error, or a batch file with a failing line.
.It 2
Refused.
Nothing was written.
.It 3
Database error: a property list would not parse, the lock was held, or a
write failed.
.It 4
Interrupted, or the confirmation was answered no.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create an administrator without being asked anything except the password:
.Pp
.Dl # adduser --admin -c "Joe Maloney" joe
.Pp
Create an account with no password, the way the image's own
.Li admin
is seeded:
.Pp
.Dl # adduser -w none -c "Kiosk" kiosk
.Pp
Provision from a script with a hash computed elsewhere:
.Pp
.Dl # adduser -q -p '$6$...' -c "Build Robot" builder
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpass 1 ,
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr login.conf 5 ,
.Xr shells 5 ,
.Xr createhomedir 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr rmuser 8
.Sh HISTORY
This
.Nm
was written for NextBSD in C, over the DirectoryServices property lists.
It keeps the name and the conversational shape the command has carried on
the BSDs since the 1990s, and drops the questions that only made sense for
a flat password file.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Joseph Maloney
